export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Rendering Optimization</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Introduction</strong></p>\n<p>Every game is a tug o’ war between performance and art quality. High-fidelity graphics are immersive, but a stable frame rate is crucial for the play experience. To manage this balancing act, we will need to learn rendering optimization.</p>\n<p>First, let’s take a look at the components of a game object.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/709977e6cd8df73d2f21.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/709977e6cd8df73d2f21.webp\" width=\"531\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/438422c22a2a47db33ef.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/438422c22a2a47db33ef.webp\" width=\"512\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>A video game object</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Mesh &amp; texture</strong></p>\n<p>Earlier we learned that game assets are made of triangle meshes. But a mesh only represent the form of an object. For an object to have meaning, we need textures.</p>\n<p>Textures are 2D images imposed on a mesh to add a sense of tactility. A texture exhibits how something feels upon touching, telling players what a surface is made of: Is it metal or wood?</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Texel density</strong></p>\n<p>A texel is the smallest unit of a texture map. However, a texel is not equal to a pixel because 1 texel can contain as many or as few pixels as you want. This is important to get the highest and most consistent texel density for your textures.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a3655d7bd6ff8d66114b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a3655d7bd6ff8d66114b.webp\" width=\"561\" height=\"273\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e3ccdda74fb0b954fc80.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e3ccdda74fb0b954fc80.webp\" width=\"529\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/80696cfb51cf4113fc24.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/80696cfb51cf4113fc24.webp\" width=\"509\" height=\"574\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>EXTRA – Why are textures powers of two?</strong></p>\n<p>Textures’ resolutions tend to fit in a power-of-two square (128x128, 256x256, 512x512, or 1024x1024). The reason behind this is to make them compatible with older GPUs (before 2006), which could only work with those resolutions.</p>\n<p>For example, on older hardware, POT textures can be stored and compressed in GPU memory while non-POT textures will be uncompressed and take up more memory.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>In this example, the red areas will require in-game resizing/scaling. If this texture is 258 x 127 height/width, there will be adjustments to turn it into 256 x 128 height/width (power of two).</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/052d4c67fe588e9e329f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/052d4c67fe588e9e329f.webp\" width=\"603\" height=\"295\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/15dd17782bc15a31295e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/15dd17782bc15a31295e.webp\" width=\"452\" height=\"565\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0d1f1f8d56e468b3c665.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0d1f1f8d56e468b3c665.webp\" width=\"568\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Material</strong></p>\n<p>In essence, materials are an upgrade of textures. They take textures as an input and turning them into a parameter which artist can adjust to customize the appearance of a surface.</p>\n<p>In addition to textures, materials accompany other parameters such as transparency and reflectivity.</p>\n<p>“The more unique materials you have, the more draw calls are required.”</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Physically-Based Rendering (PBR)</strong></p>\n<p><em>Physically-based</em> refers to realistic behavior of light. In essence, PBR is a technique to produce <strong>materials that respond to light more accurately</strong>. For example, direct light will always be stronger than bounced light.</p>\n<p>Because this type of light calculations require precise tuning of brightness, HDR-enabled display panels will produce more vibrant colors for PBR materials.</p>\n<p>Currently, the most common PBR workflow is the <strong>roughness/</strong><strong>metalness</strong> workflow.</p>\n<p>For a practical look into PBR, check out this article on stylization.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6fa13460936b23a0e108.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6fa13460936b23a0e108.webp\" width=\"621\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong> – <strong>The roughness/</strong><strong>metalness</strong><strong> workflow</strong></p>\n<p>The roughness workflow combines the albedo, metalness, and roughness maps to create a material. Although it’s also called “the metalness workflow,” it can be used for non-metals too.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6f3fa32a804b1a3fd716.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6f3fa32a804b1a3fd716.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"346\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>EXTRA – </strong><strong>Textures vs. Materials</strong></p>\n<p><em>Halo Reach </em>used traditional handpainted textures, meaning light and shadow information was manually added to the 2D images. As a result, the surface details of the gun remain pronounced regardless of the lighting conditions. Meanwhile, <em>Halo Infinite</em> (screenshot from 2020 non-final gameplay) opted for PBR materials which depend heavily on light. Because of that, a lot of the details on the gun model in the new game get lost in shadow.</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d1e5239081eff40c392c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d1e5239081eff40c392c.webp\" width=\"596\" height=\"336\" style=\"--source-width:130px\" alt=\"Illustration 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7bd07d84cac57cca14cc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7bd07d84cac57cca14cc.webp\" width=\"596\" height=\"336\" style=\"--source-width:130px\" alt=\"Illustration 12\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><em>Halo Reach</em></p></td><td><p><em>Halo Infinite demo</em></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Memory bandwidth</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What is it?</strong></p>\n<p>As you can see in the GPU pipeline diagram, meshes and textures are stored in memory. So, if the GPU needs a texture, it has to reach the RAM.</p>\n<p>This “reaching” process is similar to downloading files from the internet. The bandwidth will determine how fast or slow it takes. This bandwidth is also shared—the more files being loaded, the slower.</p>\n<p>For a game to have consistent visual quality, we need to make sure the GPU can get what it needs from the memory as fast as possible.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b10cdcc816b21c844544.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b10cdcc816b21c844544.webp\" width=\"502\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 13\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b95108d83452e651746a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b95108d83452e651746a.webp\" width=\"613\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 14\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>How to deal with it?</strong></p>\n<p>There are two common ways to deal with memory bandwidth:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Mipmaps</strong> adjust texture resolutions dynamically based on the view distance. This means not every texel has to be loaded.</li><li><strong>Texture compression</strong> can reduce the file size significantly (1/4 or even 1/6 of the original size).</li></ul>\n<p>The basic idea is to give less pixels to objects far away and more pixels to objects near the player. And the result is smoother visual presentation with lower performance costs.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>EXTRA – </strong><strong>Mipmaps &amp; powers of two</strong></p>\n<p>Creating a mipmap is basically downscaling the resolution of a texture. If we use POTs, this scaling process will be faster to compute.</p>\n<p>For example, if we have a 512x512 texture and we divide it by 2, the result will be squarely 256x256.</p>\n<p>Thanks to POT, we can easily downscale textures until we reach 1 on a dimension. For example, 256x256 → 128x128 → 64x64 → 32x32 → 16x16 → 8x8 → 4x4 → 2x2 → 1x1.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3cdae0159c5b1c89f86a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3cdae0159c5b1c89f86a.webp\" width=\"399\" height=\"574\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 15\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9faf9122b178f79a30be.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9faf9122b178f79a30be.webp\" width=\"241\" height=\"296\" style=\"--source-width:130px\" alt=\"Illustration 16\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/de2297c7cf58ae0068e6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/de2297c7cf58ae0068e6.webp\" width=\"244\" height=\"296\" style=\"--source-width:130px\" alt=\"Illustration 17\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Without filtering</p></td><td><p>With filtering</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8cca76190c6a0448ae5e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8cca76190c6a0448ae5e.webp\" width=\"483\" height=\"262\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 18\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>EXTRA – </strong><strong>Texture filtering</strong></p>\n<p>Mipmaps save performance, but they make distant textures look blurrier, especially when they are viewed from a high angle. To sharpen distant textures, we use texture filtering. This method determines the color of a texture-mapped pixel by blending the colors of nearby texels.</p>\n<p>There are two main categories of texture filtering:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Magnification</strong>: when players get close to an object, add more pixels to a single texel (increase density)</li><li><strong>Minification</strong>: when players get far away from an object, remove pixels from per texel (decrease density)</li></ul>\n<p>You can think of texture filtering as anti-aliasing for textures.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>EXTRA – </strong><strong>Texture filtering</strong></p>\n<p>These are some common methods of texture filtering:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Nearest-neighbor interpolation</strong>: duplicating the existing colors to the nearest pixels</li><li><strong>Linear mipmap filtering</strong>: sampling from two nearest mipmap levels</li><li><strong>Bilinear filtering</strong>: averaging the colors of 4 nearest pixels</li><li><strong>Trilinear filtering</strong>: interpolating two nearest mipmap levels to get the in-between result; this method makes the transition from one mipmap to another smoother</li></ul></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/bccdca8a7506813d3c10.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/bccdca8a7506813d3c10.webp\" width=\"362\" height=\"561\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 19\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>EXTRA – </strong><strong>Anisotropic filtering</strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n<p>Anisotropic filtering is the highest quality method at the moment. Compared to the bilinear and trilinear methods, anisotropic filtering enhances the sharpness of textures seen from a distance without making them too noisy. For PC games, this option can be cranked up to 16 times, which great improves visual clarity at the cost of memory bandwidth.<a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/20f2dda1a9dc70de03a9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/20f2dda1a9dc70de03a9.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"309\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 20\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Block compression</strong></p>\n<p>Block compression (BC) is the technique of cutting a texture into small 4x4 blocks. Each block is smaller in size and quicker to process, but still retains enough clarity for rendering.</p>\n<p>As you can see in the first picture, the color variation of each block is minimal. Therefore, the BC1 format just simplifies them using a four-color palette. The results are nearly identical.</p>\n<p>There are 7 different formats of BC, numbered from 1 – 7, each of which has distinct uses.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d001b6efa325234acf87.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d001b6efa325234acf87.webp\" width=\"398\" height=\"614\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 21\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>PRO-TIP</strong><strong> – </strong><strong>Block compression formats</strong></p>\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td></td><td><p><strong>Type Of Data</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>Data Rate</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>Palette Size</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>Line Segments</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>Used </strong><strong>For</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>BC1</strong></p></td><td><p>RGB + optional 1-bit alpha</p></td><td><p>0.5 byte/px</p></td><td><p>4</p></td><td><p>1</p></td><td><p>Color maps<br>Cutout color maps (1-bit alpha)<br>Normal maps, if memory is tight</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>BC2</strong></p></td><td><p>RGB + 4-bit alpha</p></td><td><p>1 byte/px</p></td><td><p>4</p></td><td><p>1</p></td><td><p>n/a</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>BC3</strong></p></td><td><p>RGBA</p></td><td><p>1 byte/px</p></td><td><p>4 color + 8 alpha</p></td><td><p>1 color + 1 alpha</p></td><td><p>Color maps with full alpha<br>Packing color and mono maps together</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>BC4</strong></p></td><td><p>Grayscale</p></td><td><p>0.5 byte/px</p></td><td><p>8</p></td><td><p>1</p></td><td><p>Height maps<br>Gloss maps<br>Font atlases<br>Any grayscale image</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>BC5</strong></p></td><td><p>2 × grayscale</p></td><td><p>1 byte/px</p></td><td><p>8 per channel</p></td><td><p>1 per channel</p></td><td><p>Tangent-space normal maps</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>BC6</strong></p></td><td><p>RGB, floating-point</p></td><td><p>1 byte/px</p></td><td><p>8–16</p></td><td><p>1–2</p></td><td><p>HDR images</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>BC7</strong></p></td><td><p>RGB or RGBA</p></td><td><p>1 byte/px</p></td><td><p>4–16</p></td><td><p>1–3</p></td><td><p>High-quality color maps<br>Color maps with full alpha</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Shader</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What is it?</strong></p>\n<p>The GPU has its own set of instructions called shaders. The two most common types are pixel shaders and vertex shaders; the former calculate the color of each pixel, the latter modify vertex position, color and texture coordinates.</p>\n<p>Because instructions take time to execute, less instructions will result in the less bottlenecks.</p>\n<p>For pixel shaders, it is <strong>choosing a simpler material</strong> to reduce instructions required to render each pixel.</p>\n<p>For vertex shaders, it is <strong>simplifying a mesh</strong> to reduce the vertex count; <strong>using LODs </strong>(Level of Detail) is also important.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8cad48e6c55445e0c5aa.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8cad48e6c55445e0c5aa.webp\" width=\"528\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 22\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3b7abb3c94bb4a9f97f7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3b7abb3c94bb4a9f97f7.webp\" width=\"369\" height=\"600\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 23\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Overshading</strong></p>\n<p>The GPU doesn’t render pixels individually; instead, it renders pixels in a <strong>2x2 pattern</strong> (a <strong>quad</strong>). So, when a triangle is so small that it only occupies 1 pixel of a quad, the GPU still processes the other 3 empty pixels, leading to 75% wasted work. This situation is called “overshading.”</p>\n<p>One way to avoid overshading is to <strong>get rid of small, thin triangles</strong>—try to keep them big and even. LODs and culling also help, but this issue can’t be completely avoided since the further away from an object, the smaller the triangles become.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Overdraw</strong></p>\n<p>Overdraw is <strong>when a pixel is re-rendered </strong><strong>over and over again</strong>. This makes the GPU spend more time on one pixel than it should.</p>\n<p>In the opposite example, the volcano smoke is re-rendered the most because it’s a see-through matter.</p>\n<p>Sometimes, overdraw is unavoidable: translucent objects revealing others, background objects visible through the foreground, etc. But it <strong>should</strong> <strong>not happen to opaque objects</strong>. To reduce this type of overdraw, programmers use early depth test.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8dfe474e413a1c3c273b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8dfe474e413a1c3c273b.webp\" width=\"586\" height=\"599\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 24\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ab13561a170e36a60e00.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ab13561a170e36a60e00.webp\" width=\"463\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 25\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Early depth test</strong></p>\n<p>Early depth test happens before the pixel shader in the pipeline; it will skip pixel shading if the pixel is hidden by another object.</p>\n<p>A function called <strong>depth buffer </strong>(or <strong>Z-buffer</strong>) will compare the triangle’s position relative to others to determine what is in the front and what is in the back.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Visibility culling</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What is it?</strong></p>\n<p>By cutting what is not visible to the player, we can reduce the number of things the hardware has to render, which in turn increases performance. Visibility culling is the specific technique for that purpose.</p>\n<p>There are 3 common ways to handle visibility culling:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Distance culling</strong></li><li><strong>Frustum culling</strong></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Occlusion culling</strong></p></td><td><div class=\"source-panel\"><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7d8419ccf2e1a43fb448.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7d8419ccf2e1a43fb448.webp\" width=\"403\" height=\"291\" style=\"--source-width:253px\" alt=\"Illustration 26\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p><p>Frustum culling</p><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/bd8472929e67b4b11ebf.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/bd8472929e67b4b11ebf.webp\" width=\"403\" height=\"291\" style=\"--source-width:253px\" alt=\"Illustration 27\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p><p>Occlusion culling</p></div></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/49027f9b31107d11986b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/49027f9b31107d11986b.webp\" width=\"700\" height=\"394\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 28\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><br><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/28f9684581166aef1304.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/28f9684581166aef1304.webp\" width=\"765\" height=\"427\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 29\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a>Cull Distance Volumes</p></td><td><p><strong>Distance culling</strong></p>\n<p>Distance culling is an option to cull objects at certain distance from the camera.</p>\n<p>In Unreal Engine 4, <strong>Cull Distance Volumes </strong>are a useful optimization tool that defines what distance to draw (or make visible) any object within the volume.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Frustum culling</strong></p>\n<p>In 3D graphics, frustum refers to the visible region on the screen. Following that logic, frustum culling basically hides everything outside the visible region (the player’s view).</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ccafc4cafdf55f915588.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ccafc4cafdf55f915588.webp\" width=\"641\" height=\"231\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 30\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3364b67992c8c143252c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3364b67992c8c143252c.webp\" width=\"800\" height=\"450\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 31\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0bbcc4ad770764976eb8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0bbcc4ad770764976eb8.webp\" width=\"467\" height=\"329\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 32\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c190bb4273e46f964a2f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c190bb4273e46f964a2f.webp\" width=\"500\" height=\"281\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 33\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Occlusion culling</strong></p>\n<p>Occlusion culling checks the visibility of all objects within the frustum to see whether they are being blocked (occluded) by something else.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Batching</strong></p>\n<p>Continuing the topic of reducing object count, we have a technique called “batch rendering.”</p>\n<p>This technique combines multiple draw calls in a batch to draw them together. With batching, we treat a group of objects as a single piece of geometry so that we can render it more efficiently.</p>\n<p>Objects must have the same material and don’t move to use static batching.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/88bd72f312ba0ce08071.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/88bd72f312ba0ce08071.webp\" width=\"521\" height=\"600\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 34\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2eac37c344c56e7e6579.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2eac37c344c56e7e6579.webp\" width=\"455\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 35\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>GPU instancing</strong></p>\n<p>Another way to reduce the object count is <em>instancing</em>. This feature allows us to instantiate identical objects using only one draw call.</p>\n<p>It’s important to note that GPU instancing <strong>can only be used on the same mesh with the same material</strong>.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Profiler</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What is it?</strong></p>\n<p>Not every problem has an obvious symptom. Profilers are very useful for identifying performance issues and resource allocation. Here, we will focus on two categories of bottlenecks:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>CPU bound </strong>— object count, draw call, culling</li><li><strong>GPU bound </strong>— shaders, overdraw, lights</li></ul></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ba16389bd795bb818a29.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ba16389bd795bb818a29.webp\" width=\"525\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 36\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f44995c3b743e0ab1218.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f44995c3b743e0ab1218.webp\" width=\"565\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 37\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>CPU profiling</strong></p>\n<p>If the rendering process is bound to CPU, the problem may be due to the number of draw calls. In this case, artists can:</p>\n<ul><li>Combine draw calls to reduce the cost (e.g. combine multiple walls into one mesh)</li><li>Reduce object count (static/dynamic meshes, particles)</li><li>Reduce view distance</li><li>Reduce elements per draw calls (combine materials or use less materials; combine small textures into larger textures; use LODs)</li><li>Disable features like custom depth or shadow casting</li><li>Etc.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>GPU profiling</strong></p>\n<p>Unlike the CPU, the GPU has many units working in parallel, so the problem could come from different places. Therefore, we need to first identify where the GPU cost is going, and where the bottleneck is.</p>\n<p>In Unreal Engine, you can use <strong>r.ScreenPercentage</strong><strong> 10 </strong>to quickly identify whether your performance issue is CPU- or GPU-bound. This command asks the game to render less pixels on screen, so if:</p>\n<ul><li>The game <strong>runs faster</strong>, your <strong>GPU</strong> is overtaxed.</li><li>The game <strong>doesn’t run faster</strong>, your <strong>CPU</strong> is overtaxed.</li></ul></td><td><div class=\"source-panel\"><p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/af7453918512c84e5912.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/af7453918512c84e5912.webp\" width=\"499\" height=\"326\" style=\"--source-width:253px\" alt=\"Illustration 38\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p><p>Unity GPU profiler</p><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/71b892e9fd7a24d2aee7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/71b892e9fd7a24d2aee7.webp\" width=\"499\" height=\"267\" style=\"--source-width:253px\" alt=\"Illustration 39\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p><p>Unreal GPU profiler</p></div></td></tr><tr><td><p>Less typical causes:</p>\n<ul><li>Object cost → likely a CPU’s problem but some GPU cost could be involved</li><li>meshes</li><li>View cost → visibility culling issue</li><li>Scene cost → GPU particle simulation</li><li>LOD</li></ul></td><td><p>Below are some well-known GPU-bound issues and how to address them.</p>\n<p>Typical causes:</p>\n<ul><li>Having too many vertices → use LOD</li><li>Having complex World Position Offset/Displacement Material using Textures with poor MIP mapping → adjust the Material</li><li>Tessellation → avoid if possible; adjust tessellation factor</li><li>Having many UVs or Normal seams might result in more vertices =&gt; look at unwrapped UV</li><li>Having too many vertex attributes → extra UV channels</li><li>If the vertex count is reasonable, some importer code might not have welded the vertices → combine vertices that have similar position, UV and normal</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>In summary</strong></p>\n<p>Making art is not easy, so is optimizing art. If you are aware of technical constraints early on, you can avoid minor issues and unnecessary iteration down the line. That awareness is acquired by studying the target platform and the game engine before sculpting and texturing.</p>\n<p>Every game is a joint effort, and the great thing about that is you always have people around to help you out. If you don’t know where to start, come talk to your technical artists and learn the hardware and software from them. Because only by working together can we make games better.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2f29b7482ec7d26920e6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2f29b7482ec7d26920e6.webp\" width=\"598\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 40\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div>";
